5. SAME-COUNTRY ELIGIBILITY ENGINE

This is a core business rule.

The system must compare canonical country codes, not country names or emoji flags.

Use ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 country codes.

For example:

ET = Ethiopia
KE = Kenya

The exact country names and codes should come from a reliable country dataset.

Required validation

For physical listings:

Borrower country = Lender profile country = Listing country.

All three must match.

Pseudocode:

if (listing.type === "physical") {
if (
borrower.countryCode !== lender.countryCode ||
listing.countryCode !== lender.countryCode
) {
rejectPhysicalBooking();
}
}

if (listing.type === "digital") {
preserveExistingGlobalBookingRules();
}

Use the existing project's language and architecture when implementing this logic.

Enforcement locations

Enforce the rule at:

- Marketplace discovery.
- Listing detail eligibility.
- Booking request creation.
- Lender booking acceptance.
- Checkout.
- Backend booking authorization.
- Any other endpoint that can create or fund a physical booking.

Never rely only on hidden buttons or frontend filtering.

A malicious user must not be able to bypass the country restriction through a direct API request.

Country change protection

Prevent country changes from silently changing the meaning of an existing physical booking.

Store immutable country snapshots for each booking.

For active physical bookings, require the appropriate lifecycle rules before allowing changes that would affect the agreement.

If a lender changes their country, existing listings must be reviewed and unpublished or updated until their location information is valid again.

Do not invalidate previously completed bookings.

6. CONTACT INFORMATION SYSTEM

Introduce a professional contact information section.

This system must work for both borrowers and lenders.

